## cc65 C — C64-specific rules and gotchas

### int is 16-bit
On the C64 target, `int` is 16 bits (−32768 to 32767). Use `char` (8-bit) wherever
possible for speed and code size. Use `unsigned char` for values 0–255 (pixel
coords, color indices, loop counters, etc.).

```c
// Prefer this on C64:
unsigned char x, y, color;
unsigned int addr;     // 16-bit for addresses

// Avoid unless you really need the range:
int big_counter;
```

### Pointers are 16-bit; zero page is valuable
cc65 uses zero page for its software stack and register variables. The `-Or` flag
enables zero-page register variables for locals — helps with pointer-heavy code.
Avoid too many pointer-type locals in a single scope without `-Or`.

### cc65 standard C64 headers — use these instead of manual defines
cc65 ships with C64-specific headers. Always prefer them over raw hex addresses:

```c
#include <c64.h>        // VIC-II, SID, CIA structs + color constants
#include <conio.h>      // screen I/O: gotoxy, cputc, cputs, cgetc, kbhit, etc.
#include <cbm.h>        // CBM KERNAL wrappers (file I/O, etc.)
#include <joystick.h>   // joystick driver API
#include <mouse.h>      // mouse driver API (1351 etc.)
#include <stdio.h>      // printf — goes to C64 screen (slow; debug only)
#include <string.h>     // memset, memcpy, strlen, etc.
#include <stdlib.h>     // abs, rand, etc.
```

Check `<cc65-install>\include\` and `<cc65-install>\include\c64\` for the full list.

### Hardware registers via `<c64.h>`
`<c64.h>` declares hardware register structs as globally accessible variables:

```c
#include <c64.h>

// VIC-II
VIC.bordercolor = COLOR_BLACK;
VIC.bgcolor0    = COLOR_BLACK;
VIC.spr0_x      = 100;
VIC.spr_ena     = 0x01;   // enable sprite 0

// SID — voices are SID.v1, SID.v2, SID.v3 (structs with freq/pw/ctrl/ad/sr fields)
SID.v1.freq = 0x0020;   /* 16-bit frequency */
SID.v1.ctrl = 0x11;     /* waveform + gate */
SID.amp      = 15;      /* master volume */

// CIA
CIA1.pra;   // joystick port 2 (read)
CIA1.prb;   // joystick port 1 (read)
```

