|----------|-------|
| `COLOR_BLACK` | 0 |
| `COLOR_WHITE` | 1 |
| `COLOR_RED` | 2 |
| `COLOR_CYAN` | 3 |
| `COLOR_PURPLE` | 4 |
| `COLOR_GREEN` | 5 |
| `COLOR_BLUE` | 6 |
| `COLOR_YELLOW` | 7 |
| `COLOR_ORANGE` | 8 |
| `COLOR_BROWN` | 9 |
| `COLOR_LIGHTRED` | 10 |
| `COLOR_GRAY1` | 11 |
| `COLOR_GRAY2` | 12 |
| `COLOR_LIGHTGREEN` | 13 |
| `COLOR_LIGHTBLUE` | 14 |
| `COLOR_GRAY3` | 15 |

### Screen and color RAM direct writes
cc65 does not pre-define `SCREEN_RAM` / `COLOR_RAM` — define them yourself:

```c
#define SCREEN_RAM ((unsigned char*)0x0400)
#define COLOR_RAM  ((unsigned char*)0xD800)

// Write a character to screen position (col, row):
SCREEN_RAM[row * 40 + col] = screen_code;
COLOR_RAM [row * 40 + col] = color;

// Fast screen clear using memset from <string.h>:
memset(SCREEN_RAM, 0x20, 1000);  // fill with spaces
memset(COLOR_RAM,  COLOR_WHITE, 1000);
```

### Charset mode — never switch away from cc65's default

cc65's C64 runtime sends PETSCII `$0E` at startup, putting the C64 in
**lowercase/uppercase charset** mode. All conio functions assume this mode.

**Do not call `cbm_k_bsout(0x8E)`** (switch to uppercase/graphics charset). It breaks
every `cputc`, `cputs`, `printf`, and `gotoxy` call — they write screen codes for
lowercase mode, so letters appear as graphics symbols instead.

If you want box-drawing borders:
- **Easy path**: use ASCII-art characters that work in any charset:
  `+` ($2B) for corners/junctions, `-` ($2D) for horizontal, `]` ($5D) for vertical.
  Write these directly to screen RAM — no charset switch needed.
- **Hard path**: switch to uppercase mode, bypass ALL conio (write every character
  directly to screen RAM with the $40-subtracted screen code), and call `cursor(0)`
  to kill the KERNAL cursor blink.

Also: if you write directly to screen RAM while conio/KERNAL is active, call
**`cursor(0)`** early. The KERNAL IRQ XORs bit 7 on the character at the cursor
position every ~20 frames, corrupting whatever you wrote there.

```c
cursor(0);   // disable KERNAL cursor blink — call once at startup
```

### conio.h — high-level screen I/O
For most output, prefer `<conio.h>` over direct RAM writes:

```c
#include <conio.h>

clrscr();                   // clear screen, home cursor
gotoxy(col, row);           // position cursor (0-based)
cputc('A');                 // put one character
cputs("hello");             // put string (no newline)
cclearxy(0, 12, 40);        // clear 40 chars at row 12
textcolor(COLOR_WHITE);     // set subsequent text color
bordercolor(COLOR_BLACK);   // set border color
bgcolor(COLOR_BLACK);       // set background color
revers(1);                  // enable reverse video
revers(0);                  // disable reverse video
cgetc();                    // blocking key read
kbhit();                    // non-zero if key waiting
```

### cc65 follows C89 — declarations must come before statements

cc65 does not support C99-style mixed declarations and code. All variable and
function declarations in a block must appear before any executable statements.
Placing a declaration after a statement causes:

```
Error: Mixed declarations and code are not supported in cc65
```

```c
// Wrong — declaration after statement:
ser_put('X');
unsigned char __fastcall__ ser_put(char b);   // error

// Right — all declarations first:
unsigned char __fastcall__ ser_put(char b);   // prototype at top of file or block
ser_put('X');
```

### Inline assembly
When you need tight 6502 control, cc65 supports inline asm with `__asm__`:

```c
__asm__("lda #$00");
__asm__("sta $d020");

// Multi-statement (each string is one instruction):
__asm__("lda #$00\n"
        "sta $d020\n"
        "sta $d021");
```

For complex sequences, put them in a separate `.s` file and assemble with `ca65`.

### Interrupt-driven code
cc65 doesn't have an `__interrupt` keyword. Hook the KERNAL IRQ vector at
`$0314/$0315` from C, or define the handler in a `.s` assembly file:

```c
// In C — redirect the KERNAL IRQ soft vector:
extern void my_irq(void);   // defined in irq.s

void install_irq(void) {
    // Disable IRQ while modifying vector
    __asm__("sei");
    (*(void(**)(void))0x0314) = my_irq;
    __asm__("cli");
}
```

```asm
; irq.s — minimal raster IRQ handler
.import _original_irq       ; chain to KERNAL if needed
.export _my_irq

_my_irq:
    inc $d020               ; flash border (debug)
    dec $d020
    jmp $ea31               ; chain to KERNAL IRQ exit
```

### Performance: `char` loops beat `int` loops
```c
// Fast — 8-bit counter, no 16-bit overhead:
for (unsigned char i = 0; i < 40; i++) { ... }

// Slow — forces 16-bit arithmetic:
for (int i = 0; i < 40; i++) { ... }
```

### Performance: avoid printf in production code
`printf` is slow on C64 (it goes through KERNAL routines). Use it only for debug
builds. For production, use `cputc`/`cputs` from `<conio.h>` or write directly
to screen RAM.

### Heap is tiny — prefer static allocation
cc65 does include `malloc`, but the C64 heap is very small by default. Declare
all large arrays and buffers as global or static:

```c
// Good — static global, known size, no fragmentation:
unsigned char screen_buf[40 * 25];

// Risky — heap is small; large mallocs will fail silently or corrupt:
unsigned char *buf = malloc(1000);
```

### PETSCII / screen codes
C64 screen RAM uses **screen codes**, not PETSCII. The mapping depends on which
charset is active:

**Lowercase/uppercase charset** (cc65 default after startup — PETSCII $0E sent by runtime):
- Screen codes $20–$3F = punctuation and digits — same as ASCII ✓
- Screen codes $41–$5A = **uppercase** letters A–Z (raw ASCII = correct screen code)
- Screen codes $01–$1A = lowercase letters a–z
- Writing raw ASCII for printable chars directly to screen RAM works without any conversion

**Uppercase/graphics charset** (switched with `cbm_k_bsout(0x8E)`):
- Screen codes $01–$1A = uppercase A–Z
- Screen codes $41–$5A = graphics/box-drawing symbols
- Must subtract $40 from ASCII A–Z to get the correct screen code

cc65 `conio.h` functions (`cputc`, `cputs`, `printf`) write screen codes directly —
they do not call KERNAL CHROUT. **However, `cputc()` maps ASCII letters in a way
that inverts case in the lowercase charset:** ASCII 'A'–'Z' produce screen codes
$01–$1A (which display as lowercase a–z in this charset), and ASCII 'a'–'z' produce
screen codes $41–$5A (which display as uppercase A–Z). Result: every letter appears
with the wrong case on screen.

To display incoming ASCII text with correct case via `cputc()` in lowercase charset,
swap the case of every ASCII letter before the call:

```c
// Compensate for cputc() case inversion in lowercase charset:
if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') c += 32;   // pass as lowercase → displays uppercase
else if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') c -= 32;  // pass as uppercase → displays lowercase
cputc(c);
```

Direct screen RAM writes do **not** have this problem — writing raw ASCII 'A' (0x41)
to screen RAM puts screen code $41, which correctly displays as uppercase A in the
lowercase charset. The inversion is specific to `cputc()`'s internal conversion.

### Keyboard input
```c
#include <conio.h>

// Non-blocking via conio:
if (kbhit()) {
    char key = cgetc();   // key is PETSCII
}

// Or call KERNAL GETIN directly via cbm.h (returns 0 if no key):
#include <cbm.h>
unsigned char key = cbm_k_getin();
if (key == 157) { /* cursor left  */ }
if (key == 29)  { /* cursor right */ }
if (key == 145) { /* cursor up    */ }
if (key == 17)  { /* cursor down  */ }
```

### Joystick — use the cc65 driver API
```c
#include <joystick.h>

// Install the standard C64 joystick driver once at startup:
joy_install(joy_static_stddrv);

// Read in your game loop (JOY_1 = port 1, JOY_2 = port 2):
unsigned char j = joy_read(JOY_2);   // port 2 is the usual player 1 port
if (j & JOY_UP_MASK)    { /* up    */ }
if (j & JOY_DOWN_MASK)  { /* down  */ }
if (j & JOY_LEFT_MASK)  { /* left  */ }
if (j & JOY_RIGHT_MASK) { /* right */ }
if (j & JOY_FIRE_MASK)  { /* fire  */ }
```

Or read CIA registers directly (faster, no driver overhead):

```c
// CIA addresses are counter-intuitive:
// $DC00  CIA1 Port A  →  Joystick PORT 2  (usual player 1 port)
// $DC01  CIA1 Port B  →  Joystick PORT 1
#define JOY2_PORT  (*(volatile unsigned char*)0xDC00)
#define JOY1_PORT  (*(volatile unsigned char*)0xDC01)

unsigned char jv = JOY2_PORT;   // bits active-low
if (!(jv & 1)) { /* up    */ }
if (!(jv & 2)) { /* down  */ }
if (!(jv & 4)) { /* left  */ }
if (!(jv & 8)) { /* right */ }
if (!(jv & 16)) { /* fire */ }
```

> **Common mistake:** writing `0xDC01` for port 2 — that's actually port 1.

### cc65 serial driver API (`<serial.h>`)

`ser_get()` is **non-blocking** — it returns `SER_ERR_NO_DATA` immediately when
the receive buffer is empty; it does not wait. Always check the return value:

```c
unsigned char err;
char c;

// Poll until a byte arrives:
do {
    err = ser_get(&c);
} while (err == SER_ERR_NO_DATA);

// Or in a loop that does other work when idle:
if (ser_get(&c) == SER_ERR_OK) {
    // process c
}
```

`ser_load_driver("filename.ser")` loads the driver from the **current disk (drive 8)**.
If no disk is mounted or the file is missing, it returns a non-zero error. Calling
`ser_open()` after a failed `ser_load_driver()` will crash (null driver pointer).
Always check the return value, or use the disk-based workflow above to ensure the
`.ser` file is on the mounted disk before the program runs.

**`c64-up2400.ser` ignores baud rate settings** — this user-port software UART driver
runs at 2400 baud regardless of the `SER_BAUD_xxxx` value in `ser_params`. Always
configure the remote device for 2400 baud and use `SER_BAUD_2400` in code. For lower
speeds via the built-in RS-232 interface (up to 1200 baud), use KERNAL serial routines
via `<cbm.h>` (`cbm_k_open`, etc.) instead of `ser_load_driver`.

**Screen scroll in a serial receive loop crashes cc65** — if `cputc()` triggers a
KERNAL screen scroll (cursor past row 24), the KERNAL scroll routine corrupts cc65's
software stack in zero page, and the program crashes to BASIC. Prevention: check
`wherey()` before each output call and `clrscr()` before the scroll can happen:

```c
if (wherey() >= 23) clrscr();   // clear before row 24 — prevents scroll
cputc(c);
```

### `#pragma` for memory placement
cc65 supports pragmas to place variables at specific addresses:

```c
// Place a variable at a fixed address:
#pragma bss-name(push, "SPRPTR")
unsigned char sprite_ptrs[8];
#pragma bss-name(pop)

// Or use __attribute__:
unsigned char __attribute__((address(0x07F8))) sprite_ptrs[8];
```

