## Meshtastic protobuf integration (nanopb)

The program uses [nanopb](https://jpa.kapsi.fi/nanopb/) to encode and decode
Meshtastic Protocol Buffer packets over the 2400 baud serial link to the Heltec
V3 LoRa radio (Meshtastic firmware).  All code is **static allocation only** —
no `malloc`.

### Files

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `proto/meshtastic_c64.proto` | Minimal self-contained proto definition; field numbers match Meshtastic wire format exactly |
| `proto/meshtastic_c64.options` | nanopb max_size constraints for all bytes/string fields |
| `proto/meshtastic-protobufs/` | Cloned Meshtastic proto repo (reference only — not used by the build) |
| `src/meshtastic_c64.pb.h` | Generated message structs and field descriptors (patched for cc65) |
| `src/meshtastic_c64.pb.c` | GCC-pre-expanded field descriptor tables (patched for cc65) |
| `src/pb.h` | nanopb runtime header (patched for cc65) |
| `src/pb_encode.h / pb_encode.c` | nanopb encoder (patched for cc65) |
| `src/pb_decode.h / pb_decode.c` | nanopb decoder (patched for cc65) |
| `src/pb_common.h / pb_common.c` | nanopb shared utilities |

### Mandatory cc65 compile defines

These three defines **must** be set in `project-config.json` under `"definitions"`:

```json
"definitions": ["PB_WITHOUT_64BIT", "PB_NO_STATIC_ASSERT", "PB_NO_ERRMSG"]
```

| Define | Why it is required |
|--------|-------------------|
| `PB_WITHOUT_64BIT` | cc65 has no `uint64_t` / `int64_t`. Remaps all 64-bit varint functions to 32-bit equivalents — safe because none of our proto fields use 64-bit types. |
| `PB_NO_STATIC_ASSERT` | nanopb falls back to `_Static_assert()` for unknown compilers; cc65 does not support it. Disabling static asserts is safe — the generator already verified field widths. |
| `PB_NO_ERRMSG` | cc65 rejects the ternary `const char* ? const char* : char*` inside `PB_SET_ERROR`. Also uncommented directly in `pb.h` line 29 as belt-and-suspenders. |

### `project-config.json` settings

```json
{
    "sources": ["src/main.c", "src/meshtastic_c64.pb.c", "src/pb_encode.c", "src/pb_decode.c", "src/pb_common.c"],
    "definitions": ["PB_WITHOUT_64BIT", "PB_NO_STATIC_ASSERT", "PB_NO_ERRMSG"],
    "includes": ["src"]
}
```

The `"includes": ["src"]` entry lets `meshtastic_c64.pb.c` find `pb.h` with a
`#include "pb.h"` — the generated file was patched from `<pb.h>` to `"pb.h"`.

### cc65 source patches applied to nanopb 0.4.9.1

Five patches were needed to make the build clean. **Do not overwrite these files
with unpatched originals.**

**Patch 1 — `src/pb.h` line 29: uncomment `PB_NO_ERRMSG`**
cc65 cannot compile the ternary inside `PB_SET_ERROR` (`const char*` vs `char*`).
Uncommented `#define PB_NO_ERRMSG 1` directly in `pb.h`. This replaces
`PB_SET_ERROR` with a `PB_UNUSED(stream)` no-op and removes the `errmsg` field
from both stream structs.

**Patch 2 — `src/meshtastic_c64.pb.h`: `<pb.h>` → `"pb.h"`**
The generated file uses angle-bracket include; cc65's search path doesn't include
`src/`. Changed to double-quote so the compiler finds `pb.h` in the same directory.

**Patch 3 — `src/meshtastic_c64.pb.c`: GCC pre-expand**
nanopb 0.4.x uses higher-order macros (`_FIELDLIST(PB_GEN_FIELD_INFO_x, ...)`)
where a macro name is passed as an argument to another macro. cc65's preprocessor
cannot handle this pattern. The file was replaced with GCC-pre-expanded output:

```bash
# Run from the project root on the Linux host
gcc -E -P -DPB_WITHOUT_64BIT -DPB_NO_STATIC_ASSERT -DPB_NO_ERRMSG -Isrc \
    src/meshtastic_c64.pb.c 2>/dev/null \
  | tail -n +459 \
  | sed 's/__builtin_offsetof (\([^,]*\),[ ]*\([^)]*\))/offsetof(\1,\2)/g' \
  | sed 's/((void \*)0)/NULL/g' \
  > /tmp/pb_expanded_raw.c
# Then prepend the #include lines and replace src/meshtastic_c64.pb.c
```

`offsetof()` and `sizeof()` are left unevaluated so cc65 computes correct 6502
struct offsets at compile time. The `tail -n +459` line number may shift if the
proto or `pb.h` changes — verify by checking where `const uint32_t meshtastic_`
first appears in the pre-processed output.

**Patch 4 — `src/pb_decode.h` + `src/pb_decode.c`: `pb_istream_from_buffer` → pointer**
cc65 cannot return structs larger than 4 bytes by value. `pb_istream_t` is 6 bytes
after the `PB_NO_ERRMSG` patch. Signature changed:
```c
/* Before (upstream) */
pb_istream_t pb_istream_from_buffer(const pb_byte_t *buf, size_t msglen);

/* After (patched) */
void pb_istream_from_buffer(pb_istream_t *stream, const pb_byte_t *buf, size_t msglen);
```
Two internal call sites in `pb_decode.c` were also updated to match.

**Patch 5 — `src/pb_encode.h` + `src/pb_encode.c`: `pb_ostream_from_buffer` → pointer**
Same struct-return issue. `pb_ostream_t` is 8 bytes. Signature changed:
```c
/* Before (upstream) */
pb_ostream_t pb_ostream_from_buffer(pb_byte_t *buf, size_t bufsize);

/* After (patched) */
void pb_ostream_from_buffer(pb_ostream_t *stream, pb_byte_t *buf, size_t bufsize);
```

### Struct size budget

All large structs must be **global or static** — never auto (stack) variables.
The cc65 software stack in zero page cannot hold them.

| Struct | Approx in-memory size | Notes |
|--------|----------------------|-------|
| `meshtastic_FromRadio` | ~300 bytes | Dominated by `MeshPacket` → `Data.payload[237]` |
| `meshtastic_ToRadio` | ~300 bytes | Same reason |
| `meshtastic_AdminMessage` | ~110 bytes | Union includes Channel (the largest member) |
| `meshtastic_MeshPacket` | ~295 bytes | Contains the 237-byte payload union |
| `meshtastic_User` | ~62 bytes | Includes long_name[40], short_name[5], id[16] |

### Usage pattern

**Note the pointer-based API** for `pb_istream_from_buffer` and `pb_ostream_from_buffer`
— these are patched from the upstream value-return signatures.

```c
#include "meshtastic_c64.pb.h"
#include "pb_encode.h"
#include "pb_decode.h"

/* Declare large structs globally — never on the stack */
static meshtastic_FromRadio  g_from_radio  = meshtastic_FromRadio_init_zero;
static meshtastic_ToRadio    g_to_radio    = meshtastic_ToRadio_init_zero;
static unsigned char         g_enc_buf[meshtastic_ToRadio_size];

/* Decode an incoming packet (after stripping the 4-byte Meshtastic serial header) */
void handle_packet(unsigned char *raw, unsigned int len) {
    pb_istream_t istream;
    pb_istream_from_buffer(&istream, raw, len);   /* pointer API — NOT = pb_istream_from_buffer(...) */
    g_from_radio = meshtastic_FromRadio_init_zero;
    if (!pb_decode(&istream, meshtastic_FromRadio_fields, &g_from_radio)) {
        return;
    }
    if (g_from_radio.which_payload_variant == meshtastic_FromRadio_packet_tag) {
        meshtastic_MeshPacket *pkt = &g_from_radio.payload_variant.packet;
        if (pkt->which_payload_variant == meshtastic_MeshPacket_decoded_tag) {
            /* pkt->payload_variant.decoded.payload.bytes = text payload */
        }
    }
}

/* Encode a ToRadio packet and return byte count */
unsigned int encode_to_radio(meshtastic_ToRadio *msg, unsigned char *buf, unsigned int bufsz) {
    pb_ostream_t ostream;
    pb_ostream_from_buffer(&ostream, buf, bufsz);  /* pointer API */
    if (!pb_encode(&ostream, meshtastic_ToRadio_fields, msg)) return 0;
    return (unsigned int)ostream.bytes_written;
}
```

### Meshtastic serial framing

The radio wraps every protobuf message in a 4-byte header before sending over
serial.  Strip the header before passing bytes to `pb_decode`, and add it when
sending with `ser_put`:

```
Bytes on wire: [0x94][0xC3][MSB][LSB][...proto bytes...]
                ^magic header^  ^big-endian length^
```

```c
/* Read one framed packet from serial into buf; returns length or 0 on error */
unsigned int read_frame(unsigned char *buf, unsigned int bufsz) {
    unsigned char h[4];
    unsigned int i, len;
    for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        while (ser_get((char*)&h[i]) != SER_ERR_OK) {}
    }
    if (h[0] != 0x94 || h[1] != 0xC3) return 0;  /* bad magic */
    len = ((unsigned int)h[2] << 8) | h[3];
    if (len > bufsz) return 0;
    for (i = 0; i < len; i++) {
        while (ser_get((char*)&buf[i]) != SER_ERR_OK) {}
    }
    return len;
}

/* Write a framed packet to serial */
void write_frame(unsigned char *buf, unsigned int len) {
    ser_put(0x94);
    ser_put(0xC3);
    ser_put((unsigned char)(len >> 8));
    ser_put((unsigned char)(len & 0xFF));
    while (len--) ser_put(*buf++);
}
```

### Connection handshake — required, non-obvious steps

Getting the radio to actually forward live `MeshPacket`s (e.g. incoming text
messages) over serial requires **both** of the following. Missing either one
leaves the connection looking superficially healthy (bytes flow, frames
decode) while the radio only ever sends small housekeeping messages
(`MyNodeInfo`, `QueueStatus`) and never a real packet.

1. **Periodic heartbeat.** The radio considers a serial client "gone" if it
   doesn't receive a `ToRadio` with the `heartbeat` field set periodically,
   and silently stops pushing `FromRadio` packets once it thinks no one is
   listening — it keeps working otherwise (messages still show on the
   radio's own screen). Send an empty `Heartbeat` (`ToRadio` field 7) every
   ~15s. Implemented in `send_heartbeat()` / `HEARTBEAT_TICKS` in
   `src/main.c`, timed via `clock()`.

2. **Resend `want_config_id` after `rebooted: true`.** The radio resets
   itself the first time a client sends `want_config_id`, and its first
   reply after coming back up is `FromRadio.rebooted = true` — not the
   config dump. The reference Python `meshtastic` CLI immediately re-sends
   a fresh `want_config_id` on seeing this. Without that resend, the radio
   sits forever waiting for a request that never comes, never reaches
   `config_complete_id`, and never starts forwarding live packets. This is
   handled in `read_serial()` in `src/main.c`: on decoding
   `FromRadio.which_payload_variant == meshtastic_FromRadio_rebooted_tag`
   with `rebooted == true`, call `send_want_config()` again immediately.

Verified via 33 back-to-back test text messages after both fixes were in
place: 35/35 arrived and displayed correctly (the extra one was unrelated
mesh traffic) — 0% loss, vs. 0 messages ever received across hours of
testing before the reboot-resend fix was added.

A large stored node database on the radio (hundreds of nodes) makes any
residual link corruption far more consequential, since the config dump
becomes proportionally larger and a single corrupted frame can cascade
(see "Meshtastic serial framing" above — a corrupted length field can
swallow several subsequent real frames into one undecodable blob). Clearing
the radio's node database is a useful diagnostic step when troubleshooting
this connection, though it is not itself a substitute for the two fixes
above.

### Regenerating the .pb files

If you add or change messages in `proto/meshtastic_c64.proto`, **all three steps
below are required** — the generator alone is not enough:

```bash
# Step 1 — regenerate with nanopb (run from project root on Linux host)
cd proto
~/.local/bin/nanopb_generator \
    --options-file=meshtastic_c64.options \
    --output-dir=. \
    --proto-path=. \
    meshtastic_c64.proto
cd ..

# Step 2 — patch the header: angle-bracket → double-quote include
sed -i 's/#include <pb.h>/#include "pb.h"/' proto/meshtastic_c64.pb.h
cp proto/meshtastic_c64.pb.h src/

# Step 3 — GCC pre-expand the .pb.c (cc65 can't handle nanopb's higher-order macros)
gcc -E -P -DPB_WITHOUT_64BIT -DPB_NO_STATIC_ASSERT -DPB_NO_ERRMSG -Isrc \
    proto/meshtastic_c64.pb.c 2>/dev/null \
  | tail -n +459 \
  | sed 's/__builtin_offsetof (\([^,]*\),[ ]*\([^)]*\))/offsetof(\1,\2)/g' \
  | sed 's/((void \*)0)/NULL/g' \
  > /tmp/pb_expanded_raw.c
# Prepend the two required lines, then copy to src/
printf '#include "meshtastic_c64.pb.h"\n#include <stddef.h>\n' \
  | cat - /tmp/pb_expanded_raw.c > src/meshtastic_c64.pb.c
```

> **`tail -n +459` caveat:** that line number may shift if the proto or `pb.h`
> changes. Verify by grepping for where `const uint32_t meshtastic_` first appears
> in `/tmp/pb_expanded_raw.c` before the pipe, and adjust accordingly. (It shifted
> to 464 after the `Heartbeat` message was added on 2026-07-04 — treat either
> number as stale and always re-verify rather than assuming.)

> **Options file gotcha:** nanopb requires fully-qualified field names including
> the proto package prefix.  Write `meshtastic.User.long_name` not `User.long_name`.
> The generator silently ignores unmatched patterns — if a max_size isn't applied,
> nanopb will emit an error about a `bytes` or `string` field without a fixed size.

